<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Port of Saint Symeon</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Port Saint Symeon, Suwaydiyah</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Port of Saint Symeon was the harbour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> at the mouth of the Orontes, near the ancient port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucia">Seleucia</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pieria">Pieria</a> and present-day Samandağ in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>. It took its name from the monastery of Saint Symeon the Stylite the Younger on a nearby mountain. Under the crusader princes of Antioch it was the main landing place for Italian merchants and pilgrims, until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> took Antioch in 1268.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/port-of-saint-symeon.jpg" alt="Port of Saint Symeon" width="320" height="367" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MapMaster · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PrincipadodeAntioqu%C3%ADa.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
